thor  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\n40:| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\n42:| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\n47:- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\n49:- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\n51:- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\n52:- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\n54:- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\n55:  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\n61:- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\n71:fixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\n73:`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n74:already observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n81:- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n93:  ends the lease.\n97:- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n98:  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n101:- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n106:Time reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n111:A tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n117:reclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n118:resource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n122:**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n130:- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n131:  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n133:A lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n134:and **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n142:A pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n146:   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n148:2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n150:   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n151:   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n152:   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n156:   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n157:   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n158:   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n162:4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n164:   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n165:   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n166:   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n168:   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n171:5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n173:   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n174:   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n175:6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n176:   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n189:Absence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n190:`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\n191:and a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\n193:`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n197:A grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n198:resource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n199:The reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n209:  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n214:  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n216:- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n224:which it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n226:extend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n234:A composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n245:- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n252:  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n253:  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n272:  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n274:  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n276:- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n281:  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n285:Proposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n290:remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n291:ordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n292:`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n293:reads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n295:reclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n300:- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n303:  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n304:  pair on a meeting peer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
