 h ctx req)\n   171\t                  more (when (and (= :dao.stream/next op)\n   172\t                                  (valid-budget? req)\n   173\t                                  (= :dao.stream/ok\n   174\t                                     (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\n   175\t                         (chase h ctx (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)\n   176\t                                outcome))\n   177\t                  answer (cond-> (assoc outcome\n   178\t                                        :dao.stream.remote/id id\n   179\t                                        :dao.stream/identity identity)\n   180\t                           (seq more) (assoc :dao.stream.remote/more\n   181\t                                             more))]\n   182\t              (write-answer! chan-writer answer))))))))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn mirror-step\n   186\t  \"The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against\n   187\t   its table: `(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) ->\n   188\t   cursor'`. For each request read from chan-reader at `cursor`, in\n   189\t   order, the four steps of 2.3 run; the mirror holds no state between\n   190\t   calls beyond the returned cursor. A value that is neither a\n   191\t   well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as malformed\n   192\t   wire input. The step stops when the channel reader answers blocked\n   193\t   or end; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery cursor, the lost wire\n   194\t   values being lost. The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites\n   195\t   a cursor, an anchor or an outcome: the served stream is the\n   196\t   original, its cursors, its positions, its gap, its end.\"\n   197\t  [table chan-reader cursor chan-writer]\n   198\t  (let [channel (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor chan-reader))]\n   199\t    (loop [cursor cursor]\n   200\t      (let [r (stream/next chan-reader cursor)]\n   201\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   202\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   203\t          (do (when (well-formed-request? (:dao.stream/value r))\n   204\t                (answer! table chan-writer channel\n   205\t                         (:dao.stream/value r)))\n   206\t              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)))\n   207\t          :dao.stream/blocked cursor\n   208\t          :dao.stream/end cursor\n   209\t          :dao.stream/gap (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   210\t          cursor)))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t;; The link (2.4, 2.5)\n   215\t;; =============================================================================\n   730\t       (= :dao.stream/remote (:dao.stream/type d))\n   731\t       (contains? d :dao.stream/channel)))\n   732\t\n   733\t\n   734\t(defn attacher\n   735\t  \"The composition's :dao.stream/attach entry for :dao.stream/remote\n   736\t   descriptors; a dynamic dispatch table has this entry and no\n   737\t   :dao.stream/create one, because attaching through this transport\n   738\t   never creates. `opts` carries the composition data:\n   739\t   :dao.stream.remote/channels maps each channel descriptor this peer\n   740\t   reaches to its own end {:reader r :writer w} -- two ring buffers in\n   741\t   one process today, the ws, udp and pair channels of section 3\n   742\t   later; :dao.stream.remote/events is the optional event writer the\n   743\t   links emit on; :dao.stream.remote/resend-after is the links'\n   744\t   resend-after k, unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small\n   745\t   on udp; :dao.stream.remote/budget is the budget stamped on every\n   746\t   next request. One link is kept per channel descriptor, shared by\n   747\t   every reflection through that channel. attach! answers ok at once\n   748\t   with a reflection handle and a local, opaque\n   749\t   :dao.stream/attachment -- the contract's deferred remote\n   750\t   confirmation -- and the lin